 ENDF/B-VIII.0 EVAL-Oct16 Brown,Nobre,Herman,Leal,Trkov,+         2634 1451
 Original ENDF/B-VIII.0 evaluation from the CIELO project         2634 1451
 The evaluation, file assembly and criticality validation         2634 1451
 were performed at BNL and IAEA by                                2634 1451
 D.A. Brown, G.P.A. Nobre, M. Herman, R. Arcilla, L.Leal,         2634 1451
 R. Capote, A. Trkov                                              2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 Primary reference: Nucl. Data Sheets 148, 214 (2018)             2634 1451
 The evaluation was performed up to 150 MeV for 7 subsequent      2634 1451
 neutron emissions, 3 proton emissions and a single alpha-        2634 1451
 particle, deuteron, triton, and 3He for a total of 146           2634 1451
 residual nuclei. Only 5 of these were treated as exclusive,      2634 1451
 i.e., had an explicit reaction assigned to them along with the   2634 1451
 corresponding exclusive spectra for all particles and gammas.    2634 1451
 The exclusive reactions were (n,g), (n,elas), (n,inel), (n,      2634 1451
 2n), (n,p), and (n, 2p). For all the remaining we provide        2634 1451
 cross sections for residua production which represent a sum of   2634 1451
 contributions from all possible reactions populating a given     2634 1451
 residue. Their respective spectra are lumped together            2634 1451
 providing only seven cumulative spectra (for gammas, neutrons,   2634 1451
 protons, alphas, deuterons, tritons, and 3He) for all the        2634 1451
 inclusive channels.                                              2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 Development of the evaluation is traced in the                   2634 1451
 CIELO-iron project on the NNDC GForge server:                    2634 1451
 https://ndclx4.bnl.gov/gf/project/cielo_iron                     2634 1451
 All elements of the evaluation, such as experimental datasets,   2634 1451
 EMPIRE inputs and outputs, intermediate ENDF files and plots     2634 1451
 can be followed there in a chronological order.                  2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 EXPERIMENTAL DATABASE                                            2634 1451
 --------------------                                             2634 1451
 The experimental database used in the evaluation is summarized   2634 1451
 in Table IX of the primary reference (NDS 148,241(2018)). In     2634 1451
 doing this selection we favored: (i) broad energy range covered  2634 1451
 by the experiment, (ii) high energy resolution, and (iii) high   2634 1451
 (but reasonable) accuracy of the experiment. Thus the            2634 1451
 measurements providing possibly wide and reliable picture were   2634 1451
 given precedence over single energy-point experiments,           2634 1451
 especially within the energy range where strong fluctuations     2634 1451
 were observed. On the other hand, many single- or a few-point    2634 1451
 measurements adopted when no extended datasets were              2634 1451
 available. Some of the measured datasets were renormalized to    2634 1451
 account for changes in the reference cross sections since they   2634 1451
 were used in the analysis of the experiments. The                2634 1451
 renormalizations built into the EXFOR retrieval system were      2634 1451
 adopted. In case of the (n,p) and (n,2n) reactions we took       2634 1451
 advantage of the existing high quality evaluations available     2634 1451
 from the IRDFF library (INDC(NDS)-0616, IAEA, Vienna (2012))     2634 1451
 and used these data instead of the actual measurements.          2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 RESOLVED RESONANCE RANGE                                         2634 1451
 ------------------------                                         2634 1451
 MF=2                                                             2634 1451
   MT=151 Resolved resonances                                     2634 1451
     The evaluation in the resonance region in based on the       2634 1451
     version of the Atlas that was still in development at the    2634 1451
     time our work was performed [3].  The compilation in the     2634 1451
     Atlas appeared to use parameters generated in Reich-Moore    2634 1451
     approximation. Because the Atlas also contained resonance    2634 1451
     parameters for resonances in the first (n,n) state, we       2634 1451
     decided to convert the Atlas compilation to the LRF=7        2634 1451
     format using Reich-Moore approximation, including the        2634 1451
     first inelastic state.  The change from MLBW Atlas           2634 1451
     parameters to LRF=7 parameters necessitated a refitting      2634 1451
     of the bound levels and R-prime. This change did improve     2634 1451
     agreement between the evaluation and experiment in the       2634 1451
     minima between resonances. The capture widths were known     2634 1451
     only from the area under each resonance. Additionally we     2634 1451
     added several resonances at the upper end of the RRR to      2634 1451
     improve agreement of the RRR with data in the region of      2634 1451
     140-165 keV.  The (n,n) resonances were determined by        2634 1451
     identifying capture resonances with no elastic               2634 1451
     counterpart and subtracting the capture width from the       2634 1451
     total width determined from (n,tot) measurements. The        2634 1451
     (n,n) resonances were assumed to be s-wave only.  This       2634 1451
     choice was supported by the experimental results of Ref.     2634 1451
     [1] and Ref. [2].                                            2634 1451
     Our thermal cross section and computed resonance integral    2634 1451
     values are in excellent agreement with the Atlas             2634 1451
     recommendations [3], as shown in the table below.  Our       2634 1451
     MACS(30 keV) value lies roughly in between the JENDL-4.0     2634 1451
     and KADoNiS-0.3 values and is generally consistent with      2634 1451
     the experimental averaged value given in the new Atlas.      2634 1451
     The Atlas value derived from systematics is more             2634 1451
     consistent with the KADoNiS-0.3 value. We note that the      2634 1451
     in-development version of KADoNiS-1.0 recommends a value     2634 1451
     basically taken from the Giubrone thesis [4], measured       2634 1451
     at CERN n-TOF. This measurement does not span the whole      2634 1451
     energy range of the resonance region. Therefore Giubrone     2634 1451
     omits several low energy resonances that would have          2634 1451
     provided a smooth background to the 30 keV region,           2634 1451
     lowering the MACS(30 keV) value obtained from the            2634 1451
     consideration of that set alone. Thus we use the MACS        2634 1451
     data for validation purposes only.                           2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
     Calculated 2200-m/s cross sections and res. integrals        2634 1451
             thermal   res. integ.    MACS (30 keV)               2634 1451
    total    3.087 b   94.48 b                                    2634 1451
    elastic  0.604 b   82.25 b                                    2634 1451
    capture  2.484 b    1.51 b       35.98 mb                     2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 REACTION MODELING IN FAST NEUTRON RANGE                          2634 1451
 ---------------------------------------                          2634 1451
 The fast region calculations were performed using the EMPIRE     2634 1451
 code, which is a comprehensive package that integrates into a    2634 1451
 single system a number of important physical modules and         2634 1451
 evaluation tools. We have adopted coupled channel (CC)           2634 1451
 calculations in the incident channel and for the direct          2634 1451
 inelastic scattering to collective levels. In addition, we       2634 1451
 have allowed for the Distorted Wave Born Approximation (DWBA)    2634 1451
 to populate presumably collective levels which are not           2634 1451
 included in the ground state band used in the CC calculations    2634 1451
 to adjust neutron emission spectra in the high energy range.     2634 1451
 For the preequilibrium (PE) emission employed the classical      2634 1451
 exciton model (PCROSS module) because of its higher              2634 1451
 flexibility in modeling neutron spectra compared with the        2634 1451
 physically more advanced, but also more rigid quantum            2634 1451
 mechanical models. The same module was also used for the PE      2634 1451
 emission of protons and gammas as well as for PE emission of     2634 1451
 clusters, the latter taking advantage of the Iwamoto-Harada      2634 1451
 model.                                                           2634 1451
 The Hauser-Feshbach formulation of the statistical model was     2634 1451
 employed to follow sequential emission of particles from the     2634 1451
 compound nucleus (CN). The elastic and inelastic angular         2634 1451
 distributions of neutrons emitted from the CN were calculated    2634 1451
 using the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism. The width fluctuation      2634 1451
 factor was accounted for within HRTW formalism applied up to     2634 1451
 12.0 MeV incident neutrons. Compound nucleus and direct cross    2634 1451
 sections were added incoherently.                                2634 1451
 Gilbert-Cameron model for level-density has been adopted         2634 1451
 for all nuclei involved in this evaluation. The parameters were  2634 1451
 fitted to reproduce the asymptotic behavior of discrete          2634 1451
 levels, as well as to correctly describe the observed cross      2634 1451
 sections.                                                        2634 1451
 Complete g-ray cascades for every excited nucleus were           2634 1451
 calculated using M1, E1 and E2 electromagnetic transitions.      2634 1451
 g-strength functions for E1 transitions from the RIPL3 MLO1      2634 1451
 formulation by Pluyko.                                           2634 1451
 Occasionally, we resorted to energy-dependent parameter          2634 1451
 scaling or tuning factors, available in the EMPIRE code, to      2634 1451
 account for model deficiencies. These corrections were           2634 1451
 typically on the level of 1-2% or less and can be found in the   2634 1451
 input files. Whenever possible the reaction model parameters     2634 1451
 used in the 57Fe evaluation were kept consistent with those      2634 1451
 used in the 56Fe evaluation.                                     2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
 FAST NEUTRON RANGE                                               2634 1451
 ------------------                                               2634 1451
 MF=3 Neutron cross sections                                      2634 1451
   EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data    2634 1451
   were adopted unless stated differently below.                  2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=1 Total                                                      2634 1451
    The fluctuations provided in the ENDF/B-VII.1 were            2634 1451
    derived from the abundance-weighted 56Fe(n,total) cross       2634 1451
    section. To  restore  the fluctuations, we performed a        2634 1451
    generalized least-square linear spline fit to the data of     2634 1451
    Harvey [5] and that of Pandey [6] in the region from          2634 1451
    190 keV to 8 MeV. Both sets were in excellent agreement,      2634 1451
    differing only slightly in their treatment of the neutron     2634 1451
    time-of-flight. At the energy resolution of our spline        2634 1451
    fit, this difference was not observable. The fluctuations     2634 1451
    were distributed to the 57Fe(n,elas) and 57Fe(n,g) cross      2634 1451
    sections such that both the energy average elastic and        2634 1451
    capture cross sections were preserved and so that they        2634 1451
    sum to the fluctuating total cross section. The data of       2634 1451
    Hibden et al. [7] were disregarded as the target was an       2634 1451
    isotopically-enriched iron oxide target and the target        2634 1451
    composition was not given to enough precision to extract      2634 1451
    meaningful data.                                              2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=2 Elastic scattering                                         2634 1451
    This evaluation is distinctly different from ENDF/B-VII.1     2634 1451
    Apart from the lower thermal region, the new evaluation       2634 1451
    adopts smooth EMPIRE calculations above the resonance region, 2634 1451
    contrary to ENDF/B-VII.1 which propagated 56Fe fluctuations   2634 1451
    up to several MeV.                                            2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=4, 51-91 Inelastic scattering                                2634 1451
    57Fe is a special case since the inelastic threshold lies     2634 1451
    inside the resonance region requiring the Reich-Moore         2634 1451
    formalism and the ENDF-6 option LRF=7, as described in the    2634 1451
    resonance section.                                            2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=5 (n,anything)                                               2634 1451
    Sum of all reactions not given explicitly in another MT       2634 1451
    number as calculated by the EMPIRE code. This MT includes     2634 1451
    majority of the reaction channels - namely all those for which2634 1451
    we do not provide a complete set of exclusive emission        2634 1451
    spectra. The reason for this unusual representation is a      2634 1451
    difficulty to disentangle contributions of different reactions2634 1451
    to the gamma spectrum from the final residue. This becomes an 2634 1451
    issue at higher incident energies when multiple reactions     2634 1451
    contribute to the production of a given residue. Note that    2634 1451
    exclusive gamma emission spectrum is required by the ENDF-6   2634 1451
    format for each reaction that is treated as exclusive. In the 2634 1451
    MT=5 representation, the cross sections can be obtained by    2634 1451
    multiplying cumulative cross section given in MT=5 by the     2634 1451
    relative yields for production of the residual nuclei given in2634 1451
    the subsections of MF/MT=6/5. Particle and gamma emission     2634 1451
    spectra are summed up over the MT=5 reactions into the        2634 1451
    inclusive spectra given in the respective subsections of MF/  2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=16 (n,2n)                                                    2634 1451
    EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data.  2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
  MT=17, 22, 28, 107, ...,849                                     2634 1451
    EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data.  2634 1451
    MTs NOT present! Included in MT=5.                            2634 1451
                                                                  2634 1451
